course: medicine
lesson: 47
title: A knock to the head in sport: if in doubt, sit them out
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your brain floats inside your skull like a jelly in a lunchbox; a hard jolt, from a head clash, a fall or even a big tackle to the body, can shake it against the inside, and that is concussion.
explain: Freya in Newcastle clashes heads going for a header and wobbles: «Howay, I'm fine man, let's gan» («howay» and «gan» are Geordie for come on and go), but the coach takes her off anyway, because the rule in UK grassroots sport is «if in doubt, sit them out».
explain: You don't have to be knocked out to be concussed, and the signs can be odd: asking the score three times, a headache, wobbly legs, feeling sick, blurry eyes or just acting not like yourself.
explain: Once you're off with a suspected concussion you stay off for the day, an adult watches you that night, and you come back step by step: school first, then sport, and no competitive matches for at least 21 days.
explain: Helmets and scrum caps help with cuts and some bumps, but no headgear can stop the brain moving inside the skull, so the same rule applies to everyone.
fact: Concussion is a brain injury caused by a jolt that shakes the brain inside the skull, and most concussions happen without being knocked out.
fact: Signs of concussion include headache, dizziness, feeling sick, blurred vision, confusion, forgetting things, wobbling and acting differently.
fact: UK grassroots sport guidance says «if in doubt, sit them out»: anyone with a suspected concussion must stop playing and must not go back on that same day.
fact: Concussion usually doesn't show on a normal scan; it is spotted from the signs.
fact: Red flags after a head knock include being sick again and again, a headache that gets worse, a fit, being very sleepy or hard to wake, weakness, or confusion getting worse, and they mean an adult calls 999.
fact: Signs can appear hours later, so an adult at home should watch the player that evening and the next day, and they should be checked by a doctor or NHS 111 within a day.
fact: UK guidance says rest for the first 24 to 48 hours with less screen time, then gentle everyday things like short walks, backing off if symptoms come back.
fact: Lying in a dark room for a week is not the advice; after a day or two, gentle activity is part of getting better.
fact: Under UK grassroots guidance there is no competitive sport for at least 21 days after a concussion, even if you feel fine sooner.
fact: A second knock while the brain is still healing can be much more serious than the first.
fact: Return to sport goes in stages: rest, everyday activity, light exercise, non-contact training, full training, then matches, dropping back a stage if symptoms return.
fact: The guidance puts getting back to school and learning before getting back to sport.
fact: Anyone who sees a sign should speak up: the player, a teammate, a parent, a coach or the referee; telling about a mate's head knock is not snitching.
fact: Scrum caps and helmets protect against cuts and some bumps but do not prevent concussion.
exercise: What is concussion? | A brain injury from the brain being shaken in the skull | A crack in the bone of the skull | A bruise on the skin of the forehead | A headache caused by too much running
exercise: Do you have to be knocked out to have concussion? | No, most concussions happen without a knock-out | Yes, always, for at least a minute or two on the ground | Yes, or it is only a bump | No, but you must have a bleeding cut
exercise: What is the UK grassroots sport rule after a possible head injury? | If in doubt, sit them out | If in doubt, play it out | Five minutes off, then back on | Play on unless they are bleeding
exercise: After a suspected concussion, when can the player go back on? | Not that day at all | After five minutes on the bench | At half-time if they feel fine | As soon as they can stand up
exercise: Which is a sign of concussion? | Asking the score again and again | Feeling a bit hungry after the game | Muddy knees and a grass stain | Wanting to celebrate a goal
exercise: Will a normal scan always show a concussion? | No, it is usually spotted from the signs | Yes, it always shows up as a dark spot on the picture | Yes, and a blood test shows it too | No, because concussion isn't real
exercise: Which is a red flag after a head knock? | Being sick again and again | A small lump that is going down | Feeling a bit bored at home | Wanting a drink of water
exercise: What should happen if a red flag appears? | An adult calls 999 straight away | Wait until next week to see | Go to sleep early and say nothing about it | Play a gentle game to test it
exercise: How long does UK guidance advise resting first? | About the first 24 to 48 hours | A whole week in a dark room | Just five minutes | No rest at all
exercise: How long before competitive sport after a concussion? | At least 21 days | At least 21 hours | The very next match | A whole year
exercise: Why wait even if you feel fine on day 5? | The brain can still be healing, and a second knock is worse | Feeling fine is a sign of a broken leg | Coaches just like to be strict | The rules are only about paperwork
exercise: What comes back first after a concussion? | School and learning | Matches and tournaments | Full contact training | Heading practice
exercise: What happens on the return plan if symptoms come back? | You drop back a stage | You skip ahead a stage | You stop the plan forever | You ignore them and carry on
exercise: Does a scrum cap stop concussion? | No, the brain can still move inside the skull | Yes, completely | Yes, if it is tight enough | Only if it is a red one
exercise: Why should an adult watch a player at home after a head knock? | Signs can appear hours later | So they can do homework together | Because players always get sick | To make sure they don't play video games
exercise: Who should speak up if they see signs of concussion? | Anyone: player, teammate, parent, coach or referee | Only the referee, nobody else | Only a doctor in a white coat | Nobody, it's the player's secret
test: arjun asks whats the score 3 times after a tackle, his mate goes «proper sus». what now | He comes off and an adult is told; confusion is a sign | He plays on, being a bit confused is normal in a hard football match | He goes in goal where it's quieter | Someone tells him the score and he carries on
test: Coach in Kent: «Five minutes on the bench, he looks fine.» A parent says not today. Who's right? | The parent: no return on the same day | The coach: five minutes on the bench is the rule | The coach, if the player says he feels completely fine | Both: he can go back on at half-time
test: Megan's been sick twice since the match and her headache's worse. «Proper rough, Mam, cwtch me» (a Welsh hug). What next? | An adult calls 999 now; these are red flags | A cuddle and bed, it'll pass by morning | A glass of fizzy pop and a biscuit to settle her tummy | Wait to see if she's sick a third time
test: Ben from Bath: «a week in a dark room with no telly? that's mid». What's the actual advice? | Rest a day or two, then gentle activity | Yes, a week of total darkness, no screens at all | No rest at all, straight back to football tomorrow | Rest for a whole month in bed
test: Callum on day 5: «I'm no' nesh, I'm fine!» («nesh» = soft about cold or pain). Can he play the cup final? | No, at least 21 days before competitive sport | Yes, if he feels fine he's healed | Yes, but only for the second half | Yes, with a scrum cap on
test: Ivy has done 4 of the 6 stages and her headache comes back at light training. What happens? | She drops back a stage and builds up again | She jumps straight to matches to test it out | She starts the whole year over | She ignores it, it's only a headache from the effort
test: Rohan: «Footy first innit, school's long». What does the guidance say? | Back to school first, then sport | Sport first, school can wait until the season ends | Neither school nor sport for the rest of the term | Both on the same day, to save time
test: Sofia hides a head bump: «don't be sus, I'm fine, eh». Her teammate tells the coach. Snitching? | No, telling protects her brain | Yes, never tell on a mate, whatever happens | Yes, unless she's actually been knocked out | No, but only the ref is allowed to tell the coach
test: Lucas: «got me scrum cap, mate, can't get concussed». Right? | No, headgear can't stop the brain moving | Yes, scrum caps block concussion completely | Yes, if he wears two | No, scrum caps actually cause concussion
test: Grace seemed grand at 3 pm but has a headache and feels sick at 9 pm. What does that show? | Signs can appear hours after a knock | She must have caught a cold at 6 pm | Headaches at night are never related | She ate too much tea
test: In a cartoon a dog gets bonked, sees stars, shakes his head and runs on. What would happen in real sport? | He'd come off for the day with suspected concussion | He'd be fine, stars mean no injury | He'd get a free kick and play on | He'd need only a plaster
test: What would happen if a player with concussion got another knock the next week? | It could be much more serious | It would knock the first one out | Nothing, the brain is already used to it | It would heal both faster
exam: Identify the principle governing the management of a suspected concussion in grassroots sport within the United Kingdom. | If in doubt, the participant is to be removed from play | If in doubt, the participant is to continue play | The participant is to be removed for five minutes only | The participant is to be removed only upon loss of consciousness
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises concussion sustained in sport? | A brain injury which frequently occurs without loss of consciousness | An injury which invariably renders the participant unconscious | An injury reliably demonstrated by a routine scan | An injury entirely prevented by protective headgear
exam: Pursuant to current guidance, determine the minimum interval preceding a return to competitive sport following concussion. | Twenty-one days | Twenty-one minutes | Twenty-one hours | Twenty-one weeks
exam: It is given that a child is vomiting repeatedly with a worsening headache following a blow to the head. State the appropriate course of action. | Emergency services are to be summoned forthwith | The child is to be permitted to sleep undisturbed | The child is to be given a carbonated beverage | The matter is to be reviewed after one week
